WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:06.188
Clients keep their own records current
through dynamic update, RFC 2136.

2
00:00:06.188 --> 00:00:09.559
Secure dynamic update
authenticates the client and

3
00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:12.776
records each name's owner
in an access control list.

4
00:00:12.776 --> 00:00:17.562
It exists only for AD-integrated
zones, which default to secure-only.

5
00:00:17.562 --> 00:00:21.663
Any authenticated user can
create a new A or PTR record;

6
00:00:21.663 --> 00:00:26.037
once a name exists, only principals
on its ACL can change it.

7
00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:29.513
Windows signs these updates with GSS-TSIG.

8
00:00:29.513 --> 00:00:31.407
The DHCP server is part of this,

9
00:00:31.407 --> 00:00:35.424
and five settings decide whether
the records stay trustworthy.

10
00:00:35.424 --> 00:00:40.575
Name ownership: a DHCP server that performs
a secure update becomes the name's

11
00:00:40.575 --> 00:00:45.243
owner, so if its failover partner takes
over, the partner can't update it;

12
00:00:45.243 --> 00:00:48.462
both partners must use
the same DNS credentials.

13
00:00:48.462 --> 00:00:53.593
DnsUpdateProxy solves ownership by
leaving its members' records unsecured;

14
00:00:53.593 --> 00:00:56.800
Microsoft warns against
it in secure-only zones.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.255
A dedicated credential is
the recommended pattern:

16
00:01:00.255 --> 00:01:03.550
one standard user account
every DHCP server uses.

17
00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:08.601
It matters most when DHCP runs on a domain
controller, which would otherwise lend

18
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:13.350
its authority over every record, and it
isn't backed up with the DHCP database.

19
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.906
Name protection creates DHCID records,

20
00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:20.863
so an update fails rather than overwrites
when another client holds the name.

21
00:01:20.863 --> 00:01:25.163
And scavenging: without it DNS becomes
a map of where devices used to be,

22
00:01:25.163 --> 00:01:30.132
and Microsoft documents that when scavenging
and lease durations disagree, you

23
00:01:30.132 --> 00:01:35.249
get duplicate records and Kerberos tickets
for one computer delivered to another.

24
00:01:35.249 --> 00:01:38.615
These settings are rarely
documented outside the servers,

25
00:01:38.615 --> 00:01:40.400
so they belong in the inventory.

26
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.990
Chapter 04 gives read-only
cmdlets for each.
